From: nullptr-t-oss <127822226+nullptr-t-oss@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 8 Apr 2026 09:18:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/lib/bpf: fix const-correctness of strchr() on a const path

CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF pulls in tools/bpf/resolve_btfids (Makefile:1446
"prepare: tools/bpf/resolve_btfids"), which links against tools/lib/bpf. That
code no longer compiles with a current host compiler:

  libbpf.c:11241: error: assigning to 'char *' from 'const char *' discards
  qualifiers [-Werror,-Wincompatible-pointer-types-discards-qualifiers]
       11241 |  next_path = strchr(s, ':');

The signature of strchr() became const-correct in C23, and newer toolchains
implement that for C17 code as a warning too. Since tools/lib/bpf/Makefile:89
uses `override CFLAGS += -Werror -Wall`, it is a hard error and the build dies
before a single line of BTF is produced.

Command line workarounds do not survive this tree:
  * HOSTCFLAGS=... is overridden by the `override CFLAGS +=` above.
  * EXTRA_WARNINGS=-Wno-discarded-qualifiers does silence libbpf, but it is
    also handed to objtool, which is compiled by clang, where that GCC-style
    option name is unknown and -Werror turns it into a different failure.

A cast is the portable fix. The buffer is written to by strchr()'s caller in
the very next statement (the path is split out into result[]), so the object
was never const anyway.

Verified: resolve_btfids builds clean with no extra flags, and vmlinux ends up
with .BTF, .BTF_ids and .debug_info sections.

Signed-off-by: nullptr-t-oss <127822226+nullptr-t-oss@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 tools/lib/bpf/libbpf.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/tools/lib/bpf/libbpf.c b/tools/lib/bpf/libbpf.c
--- a/tools/lib/bpf/libbpf.c
+++ b/tools/lib/bpf/libbpf.c
@@ -11238,7 +11238,7 @@

 			if (s[0] == ':')
 				s++;
-			next_path = strchr(s, ':');
+			next_path = (char *)strchr(s, ':');
 			seg_len = next_path ? next_path - s : strlen(s);
 			if (!seg_len)
 				continue;
